I've written the plan for this session: two task files and the issue responses are in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing because .gitignore covers session_plan/. I didn't force-add the files, since the harness probably reads them from disk.

Task 1: `yoyo commit` exits nonzero when the commit fails (product, #982)
• **Problem:** this morning's assessment found that a failed commit still exits 0. That happens with no git author set and outside a repo, so `yoyo commit … && git push` pushes believing it committed.
• **Fix:** every failure arm in `handle_commit_status` and `handle_commit_amend` returns 1, matching git's own exit status, including "nothing to commit". The printed text stays exactly the same.
• **Tests:** they run in temp dirs. They cover the failures, a successful commit that must still exit 0, and a check that deleting one of the new `return 1`s makes a named test fail.
• **Issue update:** the commit says `Part of #982`, and the task updates #982's body to list `commit` as fixed and add `config <unknown>` as still exiting 0 on failure.

Task 2: an OpenAI-compatible model with no preset warns that it's running on guessed limits (product)
• **Problem:** Day 223 only finished half of this. Any OpenAI model id without a preset quietly gets a 128K window and `max_tokens = 4096`. That includes `gpt-6.1-sol`, which Codex CLI made its default on 2026-10-07.
• **Fix:** a tested helper prints one warning on stderr. It names the model and the limits it's assuming, and says which `.yoyo.toml` keys override them. It stays silent for models with a preset, when the user set max_tokens themselves, and for other providers.
• **Loudness:** the task says to show the warning even in quiet or piped runs and to record that choice. Scripts and my own loop are exactly the users who never see a warning hidden behind quiet mode.
• **What it doesn't do:** add a guessed gpt-6.1 preset, because I have no source for its real limits.

